{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects","id":1531182,"title":"Mage progeny and effects","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-02-20T02:57:44-08:00","id":1531182,"title":"Mage progeny and effects","content":"How would each of the shape changing tribes react to mage progeny? I hope to approach this from a multiple perspective angle, and using the older rules concerning &quot;Kinfolk&quot;.<br /> <br /> I mean this question for ALL changers as well; bears and birds included, etc.<br /> <br /> How would tribes or individual members react:<br /> -a child awaken in the tribe.<br /> -an adult awakens.<br /> -a child/adult is revealed to be mage &quot;in the closet&quot;.<br /> -if a kin awakened while abroad.<br /> <br /> how easily could a mage rely on their family? How easily could a group rely on a mage relative?<br /> <br /> What kind of relationships could form from this? Not just the negative or obvious results (i.e. immediate hostility), but the uncomfortable and complicated connections life gives time to time.<br /> <br /> how would werebears reconcile with an Hermetic?<br /> how would werelions reacts to a lost kin now a Gabrielite?<br /> what if a werespider discovered their spouse was an Etherite? Or vice-versa?<br /> <br /> Food for thought,<br /> I only wish to hear your input to this quandary.<br /> <br /> P.S.<br /> a slight tangent, but how would a mage handle delirium? Just curious and found no mention in books.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27527-devilturnip","name":"Devilturnip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-20T04:27:41-08:00","id":1531184,"title":"","content":"That's.. a lot? I think the combinations of Tribes/Factions and Mage affiliations alone would be several hundred individual cases to go over, not factoring in the circumstances of the awakened individual like you asked. A discussion would be difficult without either broad general statements or looking at specific cases.<br /> <br /> As for the tangential question, Mages count as awakened and are immune to Delirium (unless incited by a specific Gift).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-20T05:44:21-08:00","id":1531187,"title":"","content":"I would agree that mages are immune to the Delirium, but not necessarily the Curse. Kinfolk neither are immune to the Curse, it affects them only to a lesser degree (as written in revised, not sure about W20 or W5).<br /> <br /> As voidshaper stated, there are dozens and dozens of individual cases and things that could be imagined. So, all we can help with are broad general statements or specific cases from the books.<br /> <br /> In my mind, there is a difference between how the community reacts and how the people closest to the mage react. Another contributing factor might be the history that they have had with mages. Another thing is how the mage practices magick. Blood sacrifices of one's own blood or enduring pain, might seem wyrmish, but isn't far from what garou might do themselves in their Rites. Ritual murder on the other hand, might not be something that fera condone, and also practices that seem harmless to humans but might be harmful to spirits.<br /> This can be true for basically any paradigm.<br /> <br /> From the books I remember 1st Editions 'Silver Crown' where Albrecht &amp; Co meet another pack, travelling the Umbra, with a Dreamspeaker (I think kin) with their pack.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-20T06:17:06-08:00","id":1531190,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531184#post1531184\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> As for the tangential question, Mages count as awakened and are immune to Delirium (unless incited by a specific Gift).</div> </div> </div> </div>I think Delirium came from the need to hide the supernatural from the mundane, that being said, wizards are already part of the supernatural, so I believe Delirium doesn't affect them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-20T07:42:18-08:00","id":1531197,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531187#post1531187\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In my mind, there is a difference between how the community reacts and how the people closest to the mage react. Another contributing factor might be the history that they have had with mages. Another thing is how the mage practices magick. Blood sacrifices of one's own blood or enduring pain, might seem wyrmish, but isn't far from what garou might do themselves in their Rites. Ritual murder on the other hand, might not be something that fera condone, and also practices that seem harmless to humans but might be harmful to spirits.<br /> This can be true for basically any paradigm.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree. Additionally, <i>any</i> affiliated Mage or Sorcerer is connected to an out-group and will have to handle the perceived or actual conflict of interest this brings. There are some combinations that will have it a little easier depending on how much aligned their focus is with their shapechanger community - a Children of Gaia kin Dreamspeaker would probably have less problems than an Euthanatos, who would have it easier with the Silent Striders instead and so on. In very broad strokes, Crafts seem to share the most overlap, Traditions somewhere in between, and Conventions the least.<br /> <br /> There are also a few cases that could well develop into major plot points - I'm sure the Bubasti would be thrilled to discover a Mage among their remaining kinfolk, to the point where the individual might have to deal with overblown expectations. Also, Skindancers are something to keep in mind I think. There's always the possibility the pressure of being an 'outsider' gets too much.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-20T08:17:25-08:00","id":1531200,"title":"","content":"I'm going to have to answer in installments, because it's a lot and starting here I'm going to leave out the Red Talons, because there are only wolves, there's no way this could happen to them.<br /> <br /> COG: At first I imagined that they would be the ones who would be most accepting, but when I looked at the tribebook, they seem to be quite fearful of mages as a general rule, I already think that they would not interact with a technological or notoriously violent tradition I'm in doubt about the others homever. Now starting from the pre-assumption that the magician awakens within their tribe, whether in childhood or already grown up, I don't think they would reject him, but they wouldn't accept him either, I think they would try to make him not be a mage, for lack of a better term, which eventually wouldn't work and he would become it anyway. Now assuming it's in a closed group where he would actually be more accepted, I think it would work as long as he has a philosophy similar to theirs, which is quite possible.<br /> <br /> Shadow Lords: This one is kind of obvious, they would be seen as a resource to be exploited, it doesn't mean they wouldn't care, after all they have their blood, but they would be cautious about passing on information, I mean, I believe that a shadow lord would be able to assume a mage's betrayal.<br /> <br /> Silent Striders: They are on point every case is different. However I think some of them would enlist the help of a mage in an attempt to study and possibly undo their curse.<br /> <br /> Silver Fangs: I can't imagine this tribe wanting to mix with mages, I mean, they are quite elitist, but I can imagine one case or another isolated for personal gain or driven by desperation. If it came out of their kinfolk, I think they would isolate him at best, or kill him at worst. If someone had a mage child, I think they would choose to hide him or leave the tribe to protect him.<br /> <br /> Fianna: They will kill him, if someone cared enough they would try to hide him from the rest of the Fianna, probably away from the tribe's eyes, perhaps the mage himself would make this decision in fear of the reaction/retribution. Depending on Caern, they could even integrate him into the team to help in some way, but still keeping him away from outside members.<br /> <br /> GoF: Not very different from the Fianna in general, but some Caerns would try to use him as a weapon like Wotan and Mjolnir's thunder and making it clear that he would not be seen as a human being in thoses cases.<br /> <br /> Stargazers: In that case I think they would see it with curiosity and caution, now if he grew up in the tribe, I see them wanting to guide them along a &quot;good path&quot; I think depending on the approach this mage choses later he would even be seen as a valuable resource to the tribe, perhaps even helping to establish new Caerns or protect those that already exist, of course other options would be for him to decide to leave the tribe believing he has another purpose or that the right thing would be to try not to use magic, except in extreme cases. A friendly reaction, in short.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27832,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","user":"Psicose","date":". 02-20-2024, 03:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27832-psicose","name":"Psicose","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27832_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2023"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"892"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-20T21:16:30-08:00","id":1531223,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Devilturnip</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects#post1531182\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How would each of the shape changing tribes react to mage progeny? I hope to approach this from a multiple perspective angle, and using the older rules concerning &quot;Kinfolk&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>By giving them a choice between Mage society and their birth family. If they choose the former, not getting invited to holiday dinner will be the least of their worries. Also, no mage is allowed anywhere near a caern, even if they are kin.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean this question for ALL changers as well; bears and birds included, etc.<br /> <br /> How would tribes or individual members react:<br /> -a child awaken in the tribe.<br /> -an adult awakens.<br /> -a child/adult is revealed to be mage &quot;in the closet&quot;.<br /> -if a kin awakened while abroad.<br /> <br /> how easily could a mage rely on their family? How easily could a group rely on a mage relative?<br /> <br /> What kind of relationships could form from this? Not just the negative or obvious results (i.e. immediate hostility), but the uncomfortable and complicated connections life gives time to time. </div> </div> </div>They'll be very popular in some respects. For all that they don't get to come near the caern, they can also share in the daily struggles of all the other facets of their relatives' existence, if they stick with them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> how would werebears reconcile with an Hermetic? </div> </div> </div>Can anyone reconcile with a Hermetic? They're the platonic ideal of &quot;Well, actually&quot; and every conviction they hold is at odds with taking most parts of W:tA at face value.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> how would werelions reacts to a lost kin now a Gabrielite? </div> </div> </div>For a historic game, I presume? Otherwise, that's a rather long time frame and no one's going to likely care. Given that Simba territory is typically in the line of fire of colonization and Gabrielites seem like the type to engage in it, poorly.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> what if a werespider discovered their spouse was an Etherite? Or vice-versa? </div> </div> </div>Werespiders are a very special case of literally not having strong emotional attachments, so... dull surprise. An Etherite would probably be torn between freaking out (Crawlerling form is probably not something anyone just gets used to) and theorizing about what discredited science defines their spouse's existence.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\ude02","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-20T23:46:46-08:00","id":1531224,"title":"","content":"I know that some books, possibly in their in-game perspective, give the mandate to never let a mage near a caern, but why? There is that idea that mages will suck dry caerns, but does that make sense? Nodes have value to mages and caerns could be regarded as nodes of a specific ressonance. Sure, Mage not allied with the garou could try to employ Prime to alter the caern to suit their needs better, which might &quot;suck the caern dry&quot; for garou and spirits of the middle umbra. If mages wanted to get rid of garou in their area, they might come to the conclusion, that the garou will move on, once their caern is destroyed, therefore making it a strategic target. Also, the Mage might with Prime have ways to use the supposedly existing spiritual energies as quintessence, therefore becoming more powerful when in the vicinity of a caern. Which is bad for an enemy mage. But all this wouldn't necessarily hold true for a mage who is also kin and allied with the garou.<br /> <br /> Garou usually meet for moots in the vicinity of the caern, not at the caern heart but within the bawn. And, going with the PGttG, which is the only non-in-game description of typical kin activities I know, the kin would be present for parts of the moot. Not necessarily for Inner Sky and Breaking the Bone, but for the Stories and Songs and to welcome back the garou after the Revel. This is an important part of knitting the community together and it wouldn't make sense to exclude the mage, especially if one wants/needs to bind him closer to the group (emotionally), as well as keeping an eye on him. Also, if the mage does use this powers to help the sept, why not let him profit from easier access to more powerful spirits and lower gauntlet. Also, garou (and supposedly kin with the merit in question) have an easier time meditating for Gnosis renewal when at a caern, so extracting some of the spiritual energies of a caern is not unheard of for garou, either.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, what would be gained if the kin had a sanctum somewhere in the protectorate and would try to communicate with the spirits of his ancestors by summoning them to his sanctum, possibly away from the caern?<br /> <br /> I get that this &quot;don't let them near the caern&quot; mentality was introduced into Werewolf-lore as a general approach to mages. But it isn't explained in-game, iirc, apart from the vague dangers that the mages might harm the caern. So, this practice might be a remanent of time gone by, when mages were the protectors and eventually ruling caste of the human settlements, protecting them during the Impergium and in the ages after that. Does it hold up today? Maybe, maybe not. It comes down to how careful you want to be with the &quot;Don't cause a caern to be violated&quot; tenet of the Litany.<br /> <br /> But there are different approaches. I vaguely remember a story: The Hengeyokai have a similar rule about dragon nests, still there is a story where they decided to have a meeting with all local powers, including eastern vampires, mages, fae and so on, to debate if outsiders (get of fenris in that case) should be granted access to the dragon nest. So they even decided to invite their enemies to a diplomatic gathering, much to the frustration of the get of Fenris.<br /> <br /> So, I guess, there is a bit of different approaches on what exactly handle a mage at a caern and in general how fragile or resilient a caern is. Sure, the mage can't be allowed to exploit the caern, but neither can any garou. But to bar the mage kin from the caern doesn't make too much sense, since he already might know where it is (if he grew up in the sept before he awakened), and might have means to access it anyway, using spirit/correspondence, even from afar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1008,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","user":"heinrich","date":". 02-21-2024, 02:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T00:55:10-08:00","id":1531225,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531224#post1531224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know that some books, possibly in their in-game perspective, give the mandate to never let a mage near a caern, but why? There is that idea that mages will suck dry caerns, but does that make sense? Nodes have value to mages and caerns could be regarded as nodes of a specific ressonance. Sure, Mage not allied with the garou could try to employ Prime to alter the caern to suit their needs better, which might &quot;suck the caern dry&quot; for garou and spirits of the middle umbra. If mages wanted to get rid of garou in their area, they might come to the conclusion, that the garou will move on, once their caern is destroyed, therefore making it a strategic target. Also, the Mage might with Prime have ways to use the supposedly existing spiritual energies as quintessence, therefore becoming more powerful when in the vicinity of a caern. Which is bad for an enemy mage. But all this wouldn't necessarily hold true for a mage who is also kin and allied with the garou.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mages have been known to drain and damage them by treating them as just another node, so they get killed on sight accordingly. This is one of the few things that Garou and Fera definitively know mages do, and accordingly colored their view of them over the millennia.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Garou usually meet for moots in the vicinity of the caern, not at the caern heart but within the bawn. And, going with the PGttG, which is the only non-in-game description of typical kin activities I know, the kin would be present for parts of the moot. Not necessarily for Inner Sky and Breaking the Bone, but for the Stories and Songs and to welcome back the garou after the Revel. This is an important part of knitting the community together and it wouldn't make sense to exclude the mage, especially if one wants/needs to bind him closer to the group (emotionally), as well as keeping an eye on him. Also, if the mage does use this powers to help the sept, why not let him profit from easier access to more powerful spirits and lower gauntlet. Also, garou (and supposedly kin with the merit in question) have an easier time meditating for Gnosis renewal when at a caern, so extracting some of the spiritual energies of a caern is not unheard of for garou, either. </div> </div> </div>Because the capability and willingness to drain them means that they can't have nice things. Letting a mage near a caern is accordingly a violation of the final tenet of the litany, which is one of the two that every Garou takes fucking seriously.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> On the other hand, what would be gained if the kin had a sanctum somewhere in the protectorate and would try to communicate with the spirits of his ancestors by summoning them to his sanctum, possibly away from the caern? </div> </div> </div>Not much, since any Garou with technical competence in their culture can also summon spirits. Mages require a significant investment in Spirit to do this.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I get that this &quot;don't let them near the caern&quot; mentality was introduced into Werewolf-lore as a general approach to mages. But it isn't explained in-game, iirc, apart from the vague dangers that the mages might harm the caern. So, this practice might be a remanent of time gone by, when mages were the protectors and eventually ruling caste of the human settlements, protecting them during the Impergium and in the ages after that. Does it hold up today? Maybe, maybe not. It comes down to how careful you want to be with the &quot;Don't cause a caern to be violated&quot; tenet of the Litany.<br /> <br /> But there are different approaches. I vaguely remember a story: The Hengeyokai have a similar rule about dragon nests, still there is a story where they decided to have a meeting with all local powers, including eastern vampires, mages, fae and so on, to debate if outsiders (get of fenris in that case) should be granted access to the dragon nest. So they even decided to invite their enemies to a diplomatic gathering, much to the frustration of the get of Fenris. </div> </div> </div>The prominence of Hungry Dead and fae helps to keep the mages restrained, so it's a rather different setup from start to finish, there. Mages know that if they mess with the sacred sites, they'll bring down not just the Beast Courts but ancient angry undead and also the errant servants of heaven, and possibly also the Shih, who would act to keep the monsters from having a free for all by simply just killing the offending Namebreakers.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, I guess, there is a bit of different approaches on what exactly handle a mage at a caern and in general how fragile or resilient a caern is. Sure, the mage can't be allowed to exploit the caern, but neither can any garou. But to bar the mage kin from the caern doesn't make too much sense, since he already might know where it is (if he grew up in the sept before he awakened), and might have means to access it anyway, using spirit/correspondence, even from afar. </div> </div> </div>He might know where it is but he'll also know that the Warder and Guardians will be the first to kill him if he tries anything. This keeps more reasonable people from considering it overly much, and ultimately tends to keep them from doing it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T01:37:37-08:00","id":1531226,"title":"","content":"Whenever we go through the Tenets of the Litany and explain them to cubs (which happens often in an ongoing LARP game), there is the question, what is allowed and what not, in regards to the &quot;violating the caern&quot; tenet. The most often asked question is a scenario where either the majority of the sept leave for an attack, leaving the caern potentially vulnerable. The second most often asked scenario is if the sept is attacked, how far can the Warder or Sept Leader let the enemy come in to effectively mount an ambush or get into another tactically better position to start the counterattack. Often, it seems, the questions can't really be answered and also often it seems like 'success makes right'. If the enemy that was allowed in to be dealt with in the best possible way, didn't have a chance to cause harm to the caern, then all is fine - since risking alone doesn't seem to violate the tenet. If these enemies managed to cause harm to the caern, however, than the decision was bad and a violation to the Litany.<br /> But the Litany is a collection of stories from which the tenets are derived, so there might be stories a Galliard might tell you, when garou were punished even for putting the caern in a potential danger. Also, there might be plenty of stories where the garou happily died in an uneven battle, instead of offering the caern as bait, and couldn't sustain the sept and caern any more a decade later and it died anyway.<br /> <br /> For a mage, or even any other supernatural kinfolk, the question would be the same and equally hard to answer - I think.<br /> <br /> A kinfolk who comes to the sept from the outside, totally awoken, possibly as entourage to a pack that plans to settle in the sept, things are different then with the son of the sept leader, who grew up in the sept. He basically lived in the bawn or &quot;on the caern&quot; his whole life. Being there every moot since the toddler age and often even in between, when the sept leader had business at the caern. Such a person isn't sniffed out by &quot;Scent of the true form&quot; every time and suddenly someone realises 'he smells like the namebreaker I once met' (if anyone in the sept even met one). If such a child awakens, which most often is a process, the sept might not realise right away and might set another set of boundaries in the process.<br /> <br /> I mean, sure, the Ritemaster or Warder/Guardian would probably never let even the sept leader's son mage to the heart of the caern, but into the bawn? Seems a bit insincere to prohibit that.<br /> As was pointed out in the 1st answer to the original post, that we could answer the topic only in the most general ways.<br /> <br /> Also, sorcerers with mana manipulation should be restricted, if found out what they are and potentially can do.<br /> That said, the Ritemaster also should make sure that nobody else gets access to the caern's heart that shouldn't. Like performing a Rite without proper skill (not having a good dice pool) is something that could harm the caern, and is therefore something that the garou should not allow, as a community. But even accomplished garou can roll a botch at the worst of times....<br /> <br /> So, I would think, the best approach to the whole &quot;caern to be violated&quot; tenet is to act reasonable - honour and wisdom should dictate the course of action.<br /> And with that in mind, the garou wouldn't allow a mage to experiment with the caern heart, surely, but might him to visit the moots or the shrines of the fallen heroes, which are his ancestors...<br /> <br /> Simply because there is a difference between &quot;That mage guy that came with that new pack&quot; and &quot;The guy I grew up with, who is undergoing some change, much like I did when I had my first change not so long ago.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1008,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","user":"heinrich","date":". 02-21-2024, 04:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T05:39:37-08:00","id":1531232,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531226#post1531226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I would think, the best approach to the whole &quot;caern to be violated&quot; tenet is to act reasonable - honour and wisdom should dictate the course of action.<br /> And with that in mind, the garou wouldn't allow a mage to experiment with the caern heart, surely, but might him to visit the moots or the shrines of the fallen heroes, which are his ancestors...</div> </div> </div> </div>While I agree that would be the sensible course of action, shapechanger laws and Garou Nation laws in particular are loaded with the solidified gunk of millennia. The cultural segregation of full changers and kinfolk (and especially kinfolk Mages and Sorcerers) when it comes to their core duties is likely the result of negative experiences of having let lose a kid into the candy store one time too often combined with outside attacks as you mentioned earlier in the thread. And when it comes to something as invaluable as a caern, I can see why they simply won't take the risk.<br /> <br /> This doesn't sit well with modern sensibilities and most current generation kinfolk are probably not going to like that parts of their families are entitled to privilege they are denied simply because they pulled the losing ticket in the birth lottery. It also tells them directly 'you can not be trusted because you were born different', which is always a great feeling. I mean, Skindancers are still a thing after Sam Haight for a reason.<br /> <br /> I consider the many, many, many foolish and outdated laws and customs in the game integral, because they provide immediate points of friction for PCs that are not as easily resolvable as pulping wyrm spawn and forces them to make hard choices. Depends on what kind of game you want to play, of course.<br /> <br /> Some Fera might differ in this. While Ananasi would see a kinfolk Mage as a useful tool at best, I think they would have also little qualms using said tool when dealing with a caern directly.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Custom Sorcery: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1487084-pattern-weaving-ananasi-sorcery-homebrew\" target=\"_blank\">For Ananasi</a> | Mortal:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1509365-hekau-for-sorcerers\" target=\"_blank\"> Hekau</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1550552-ma%C5%A1ma%C5%A1%C5%A1%C5%ABtu-mesopotamian-sorcery\" target=\"_blank\">Ma\u0161ma\u0161\u0161\u016btu</a> | Necromancy:<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1514352\" target=\"_blank\"> Dust Path</a>, <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1535508-dav20-older-lamia-lore?p=1536244#post1536244\" target=\"_blank\">P.o.Fertile Grave Soil</a> | Thauma: <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1513911-have-you-your-players-ever-used-disciplines-sorcery-for-scholarly-pursuits/page2#post1515316\" target=\"_blank\">Ars Notoria</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27477-voidshaper","name":"voidshaper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27477_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"698"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T07:47:25-08:00","id":1531234,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531226#post1531226\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Whenever we go through the Tenets of the Litany and explain them to cubs (which happens often in an ongoing LARP game), there is the question, what is allowed and what not, in regards to the &quot;violating the caern&quot; tenet. The most often asked question is a scenario where either the majority of the sept leave for an attack, leaving the caern potentially vulnerable.</div> </div> </div> </div>You can't stay on the defensive forever. That's not really strategically viable. Most tribes have different answers to this - CoG will rely on human kinfolk to shore up numbers, Shadow Lords will typically have a scheme set up so that their enemies attack each other and end up weakening two or more foes so that they're easier to fight, and Bone Gnawers might charge the Rabble with some of the defense.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The second most often asked scenario is if the sept is attacked, how far can the Warder or Sept Leader let the enemy come in to effectively mount an ambush or get into another tactically better position to start the counterattack. </div> </div> </div>That depends on who's filling some of the other positions and what they can do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Often, it seems, the questions can't really be answered and also often it seems like 'success makes right'. </div> </div> </div>That's asymmetric warfare for you. In earlier times, the Garou had much more favorable numbers to fill out sept positions and had more options available at each potential stage of combat. Now they mostly have to rely on misdirection, which chafes them so.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> If the enemy that was allowed in to be dealt with in the best possible way, didn't have a chance to cause harm to the caern, then all is fine - since risking alone doesn't seem to violate the tenet. If these enemies managed to cause harm to the caern, however, than the decision was bad and a violation to the Litany. </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But the Litany is a collection of stories from which the tenets are derived, so there might be stories a Galliard might tell you, when garou were punished even for putting the caern in a potential danger. Also, there might be plenty of stories where the garou happily died in an uneven battle, instead of offering the caern as bait, and couldn't sustain the sept and caern any more a decade later and it died anyway. For a mage, or even any other supernatural kinfolk, the question would be the same and equally hard to answer - I think. </div> </div> </div>No, the Garou laws were meant for Garou. They don't expect that mages will be ready to die to fulfill them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> A kinfolk who comes to the sept from the outside, totally awoken, possibly as entourage to a pack that plans to settle in the sept, things are different then with the son of the sept leader, who grew up in the sept. He basically lived in the bawn or &quot;on the caern&quot; his whole life. Being there every moot since the toddler age and often even in between, when the sept leader had business at the caern. Such a person isn't sniffed out by &quot;Scent of the true form&quot; every time and suddenly someone realises 'he smells like the namebreaker I once met' (if anyone in the sept even met one). If such a child awakens, which most often is a process, the sept might not realise right away and might set another set of boundaries in the process. </div> </div> </div>Namebreaker is an appellation that the Beast Courts use, incidentally.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I mean, sure, the Ritemaster or Warder/Guardian would probably never let even the sept leader's son mage to the heart of the caern, but into the bawn? Seems a bit insincere to prohibit that.<br /> As was pointed out in the 1st answer to the original post, that we could answer the topic only in the most general ways. </div> </div> </div>Politically speaking, the Sept Leader probably won't be the Sept Leader for very long if they keep the mage around, one way or another.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, sorcerers with mana manipulation should be restricted, if found out what they are and potentially can do.<br /> That said, the Ritemaster also should make sure that nobody else gets access to the caern's heart that shouldn't. Like performing a Rite without proper skill (not having a good dice pool) is something that could harm the caern, and is therefore something that the garou should not allow, as a community. But even accomplished garou can roll a botch at the worst of times....<br /> <br /> So, I would think, the best approach to the whole &quot;caern to be violated&quot; tenet is to act reasonable - honour and wisdom should dictate the course of action.<br /> And with that in mind, the garou wouldn't allow a mage to experiment with the caern heart, surely, but might him to visit the moots or the shrines of the fallen heroes, which are his ancestors...<br /> <br /> Simply because there is a difference between &quot;That mage guy that came with that new pack&quot; and &quot;The guy I grew up with, who is undergoing some change, much like I did when I had my first change not so long ago.&quot; </div> </div> </div>The changes in being a mage are nothing like becoming Garou in a way that Garou would recognize. Sure, they might be able to speak to spirits, but they don't know Rage or feel the presence of Gaia that defines having Gnosis, and in most cases, they can't stand up to the kind of punishment that defines the life of the Garou without getting heaps of Paradox for it. They also tend to bring bad outcomes to whatever they do via Paradox, which is something that most septs aren't going to allow to happen anywhere near a caern, which can only be accomplished by not letting them be anywhere near it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T10:29:24-08:00","id":1531236,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>voidshaper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531232#post1531232\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">While I agree that would be the sensible course of action, shapechanger laws and Garou Nation laws in particular are loaded with the solidified gunk of millennia. The cultural segregation of full changers and kinfolk (and especially kinfolk Mages and Sorcerers) when it comes to their core duties is likely the result of negative experiences of having let lose a kid into the candy store one time too often combined with outside attacks as you mentioned earlier in the thread. And when it comes to something as invaluable as a caern, I can see why they simply won't take the risk.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, Fianna 1st mentioned Verbena among their kin, but then again, 1st Edition TBs are probably not the best source for how WtA is supposed to work in the more refined revised or W20 eras. On the other hand W20 Kinfolk kind of extends on magic being used by kinfolk, not to the degree a sorcerer would have, but Rites and Gifts seem to be more common that revised and 2nd had previously made it seem.<br /> <br /> Then, if a mage had no connection to other mages, but is what MtA would call a Craft, self-taught by the Avatar and possibly Ancestor spirits, using garou-like foci to do magick - sure it might also foster to old grudges and preconceptions. And, do to the liabilities of &quot;Scent of the true form&quot; the mage would still be recognised as a mage (if there was a basis for comparison). But, thematically, the kin mage with the WtA paradigm might not easily be discernable from a kin sorcerer with a similar paradigm. For &quot;Sense Magic&quot; they might be different, for &quot;Sense the unnatural&quot; they might feel quite similar...<br /> <br /> And, sure, the garou society is full of old rules that are &quot;loaded with the solidified gunk of millennia.&quot; And I get that there are a lot of animosities towards mages in general. I wouldn't dispute this as a general way the Setting works. But there are different types of septs. A close knit group with their kinfolk being integrated and integral to their every day garou life to function would react differently to one of them awakening than a larger sept, with garou from various families and possibly 50+ associated kinfolk families or couples that have varying levels of closeness to the sept's garou.<br /> <br /> As for the differences in awakening and the first change. Sure, they are different in for an external observer, but they have similarities on a personal level. One's self changes fundamentally, senses open to new levels and constrains inherit of the the former self are no longer valid. Also, once the mage could enter the Penumbra, Paradox isn't an issue any more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T10:42:26-08:00","id":1531237,"title":"","content":"The downside to going into the Penumbra to avoid Paradox is that you are now in the Umbra and it isn't necessarily a great place for a mage. Naturae don't necessarily take kindly to humans, and if you're using W:tA spirit rules, they do aggravated damage without the need of any Charms. It's like going into a garden with machineels planted everywhere. Paradox can also become a problem if a rival Garou,perhaps even a BSD or Unchanging drone, decides to summon a Paradox-spirit to go after the kin mage (and Ananasi of their mystic splat can also whistle Paradox-spirits up on demand, in only a few seconds).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T11:20:24-08:00","id":1531238,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531237#post1531237\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Naturae don't necessarily take kindly to humans</div> </div> </div> </div>Is there a reference for that?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T12:28:26-08:00","id":1531241,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531238#post1531238\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is there a reference for that?</div> </div> </div> </div>The text of it suggests that they wouldn't be particularly happy about the people responsible for devastating the Umbra and turning it into a barren and featureless slumbering land. City-spirits might be favorably inclined, but not wilderness-spirits.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-21T12:29:00-08:00","id":1531242,"title":"","content":"The text being W20 page 368.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-26T23:16:59-08:00","id":1531596,"title":"","content":"I appreciate all the correspondence on this topic.<br /> <br /> I understand that this a very broad question. This is why I wanted to spur a discussion.<br /> <br /> With SO many variables, i wanted to get some form of groundwork perspective. From multiple angles if possible.<br /> <br /> How the traditions and conventions would react is point of my lak of perspective. I often run Society of Ether who are based on academic institutions, and are therefore often decentralized. SoE technmages are usually left to their own research pursuits (save extreme cases).<br /> <br /> i hadn't considered a mages fellows may hamper relations with othersupernatural. Seem obvious in hindsight considering the Hermetics history with Vampires.<br /> <br /> However, one might also consider mages that may run, somewhat, counter the their paradigms commonality.<br /> <br /> For instance:<br /> A Son of Ether developing technology that promotes plant growth?<br /> A Virtual Adept that is recreating nature in the digital world.<br /> A Verbena that creates plants that grow from synthetic materials in an attempt to &quot;naturalize&quot; the modern cities.<br /> A Hermetic that wants to try the Tremere idea but with werewolves.<br /> <br /> It gos without saying that these examples would get ODD reactions from all sides, but WHAT exactly. What kind of story or drama could be spawned from such &quot;counter-currrent swimmers&quot;?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":27527,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27527-devilturnip","user":"Devilturnip","date":". 02-27-2024, 02:37 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27527-devilturnip","name":"Devilturnip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-27T02:41:31-08:00","id":1531607,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>heinrich</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531224#post1531224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I know that some books, possibly in their in-game perspective, give the mandate to never let a mage near a caern, but why? There is that idea that mages will suck dry caerns, but does that make sense? Nodes have value to mages and caerns could be regarded as nodes of a specific ressonance. Sure, Mage not allied with the garou could try to employ Prime to alter the caern to suit their needs better, which might &quot;suck the caern dry&quot; for garou and spirits of the middle umbra. If mages wanted to get rid of garou in their area, they might come to the conclusion, that the garou will move on, once their caern is destroyed, therefore making it a strategic target. Also, the Mage might with Prime have ways to use the supposedly existing spiritual energies as quintessence, therefore becoming more powerful when in the vicinity of a caern. Which is bad for an enemy mage. But all this wouldn't necessarily hold true for a mage who is also kin and allied with the garou.<br /> <br /> Garou usually meet for moots in the vicinity of the caern, not at the caern heart but within the bawn. And, going with the PGttG, which is the only non-in-game description of typical kin activities I know, the kin would be present for parts of the moot. Not necessarily for Inner Sky and Breaking the Bone, but for the Stories and Songs and to welcome back the garou after the Revel. This is an important part of knitting the community together and it wouldn't make sense to exclude the mage, especially if one wants/needs to bind him closer to the group (emotionally), as well as keeping an eye on him. Also, if the mage does use this powers to help the sept, why not let him profit from easier access to more powerful spirits and lower gauntlet. Also, garou (and supposedly kin with the merit in question) have an easier time meditating for Gnosis renewal when at a caern, so extracting some of the spiritual energies of a caern is not unheard of for garou, either.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, what would be gained if the kin had a sanctum somewhere in the protectorate and would try to communicate with the spirits of his ancestors by summoning them to his sanctum, possibly away from the caern?<br /> <br /> I get that this &quot;don't let them near the caern&quot; mentality was introduced into Werewolf-lore as a general approach to mages. But it isn't explained in-game, iirc, apart from the vague dangers that the mages might harm the caern. So, this practice might be a remanent of time gone by, when mages were the protectors and eventually ruling caste of the human settlements, protecting them during the Impergium and in the ages after that. Does it hold up today? Maybe, maybe not. It comes down to how careful you want to be with the &quot;Don't cause a caern to be violated&quot; tenet of the Litany.<br /> <br /> But there are different approaches. I vaguely remember a story: The Hengeyokai have a similar rule about dragon nests, still there is a story where they decided to have a meeting with all local powers, including eastern vampires, mages, fae and so on, to debate if outsiders (get of fenris in that case) should be granted access to the dragon nest. So they even decided to invite their enemies to a diplomatic gathering, much to the frustration of the get of Fenris.<br /> <br /> So, I guess, there is a bit of different approaches on what exactly handle a mage at a caern and in general how fragile or resilient a caern is. Sure, the mage can't be allowed to exploit the caern, but neither can any garou. But to bar the mage kin from the caern doesn't make too much sense, since he already might know where it is (if he grew up in the sept before he awakened), and might have means to access it anyway, using spirit/correspondence, even from afar.</div> </div> </div> </div>This actually brings up a question. One I hesitated to ask initially. Wasn't sure if it belonged in the MTA section or not.<br /> <br /> Is a mages presence pure detrimental to a caern?<br /> Given what a fully realized spirit sphere can do; could a mage cultivate a caern?<br /> Could a mage CREATE a caern? Ive read exerpts in which some archmages created their own GODs for pocket realms they created. Sure manipulating a caern for the benefit of garou.<br /> <br /> I understand it would need for the mage to somwhat understand a garous connection too caerns, and properly incorporate this knowledge into their paradigm along the garou's beliefs. <br /> <br /> farfetched, but with potential. Imagine.<br /> <br /> what if: A cabal of mages organize and capture a caern with intention to &quot;enhance&quot; it and the garou using it. <br /> <br /> given the information presented:<br /> -this could essentially make the cearn useless to wolves. They leave. Boring ending.<br /> -the mages succeed, supposedly enhancing the caern and garou. As well as likely alienating them from other tribes.<br /> -the mages fail horribly and warp the caern and tribe. Essentially creating a new BSD offshoot.<br /> <br /> each of these is a viable avenue for story creation. Whether as the misguided mage or home defense werewolf.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27527-devilturnip","name":"Devilturnip","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27527_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"19"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-02-27T05:14:45-08:00","id":1531611,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Devilturnip</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1531182-mage-progeny-and-effects?p=1531607#post1531607\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is a mages presence pure detrimental to a caern?</div> </div> </div> </div>Not inherently. Mages don't passively suck up a Caern's energy just by being near it. At worst, given how much Garou rely on their relationships with spirits to do everything, a mage could be detrimental passively from that. But if allowed, they could also do lots of beneficial things for the Caern and things that would help appease any spirits not happy about them being around too.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Given what a fully realized spirit sphere can do; could a mage cultivate a caern? </div> </div> </div>&quot;Cultivate&quot; is a fairly vague word to address this specifically.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Could a mage CREATE a caern? </div> </div> </div>No. A mage could create a Node that is attuned well to the needs of the Garou, and even help attract a spirit to bond with the Node, but the Garou would still have to use their own rituals to sanctify it as a Caern for their purposes. Though IIRC it would make life much easier on the Garou since there should be easier Rites than Caern Building to claim an existing one that's not properly aligned rather than having to go through the process of making one from scratch.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> -this could essentially make the cearn useless to wolves. They leave. Boring ending.<br /> -the mages succeed, supposedly enhancing the caern and garou. As well as likely alienating them from other tribes.<br /> -the mages fail horribly and warp the caern and tribe. Essentially creating a new BSD offshoot. </div> </div> </div>These are all rather extreme results. Breaking a Caern to the point of requiring it to be fixed is a lot more likely than breaking it beyond the point of ever being used as a Caern again. Lots of Septs have allied with other supernaturals over the stories in the books to various results; including going well. Alienating whole Tribes because a risky alliance worked on one local spot is a bit much even for how much the Garou default to hating mages. The bond between the Garou and their Caerns isn't so strong that some massive Paradox backlash would cause anything like warping whole Tribes. Besides making them really angry, it wouldn't twist the local Garou in a metaphysical sense. The worst types of Paradox smack the mages, not the target of their magic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}